51. Diversification, Vertical Integration and Merger
Paper: Industrial Economics (ECNDSE303) | Unit: II | Topic: Industrial expansion – diversification, vertical integration and merger: meaning, types, motives, measurement
Introduction
Firms expand internally by investing in capacity or externally through acquisitions and mergers. Three major strategies are diversification, vertical integration and mergers.
Diversification
Meaning: expansion into new products or markets different from the firm's current activities.
Types: related (concentric) diversification builds on existing technology or marketing; unrelated (conglomerate) diversification enters unconnected businesses; also horizontal and vertical diversification.
Motives: spreading risk, using surplus resources and managerial capacity (Penrose), exploiting economies of scope, escaping slow-growth industries, internal capital markets, tax advantages, and managerial empire-building.
Vertical integration
Meaning: combining successive stages of production under one firm. Backward integration moves toward inputs; forward integration moves toward customers; full or partial (tapered).
Motives: avoiding hold-up and transaction costs (Williamson, Coase), securing supply and demand, eliminating markups (double marginalisation), exploiting technological interdependence, entry barriers, price discrimination, and tax reasons. Costs: loss of flexibility, bureaucracy, higher fixed costs and capital needs.
Merger
Meaning: combining of two or more firms into one. Types: horizontal (same industry; increases market share), vertical (supplier-buyer), conglomerate (unrelated), and concentric. Legal forms: merger/amalgamation, acquisition (takeover), consolidation. Hostile versus friendly.
Motives: economies of scale and synergy; market power; growth faster than organic; acquiring technology or brands; eliminating inefficient management (market for corporate control); tax benefits; risk reduction; empire-building. Empirical evidence usually shows that acquirers' shareholders earn little or negative returns, while target shareholders gain.
Measurement approaches
• Diversification: Herfindahl index of sales across industries, Entropy measure (total, related and unrelated), Berry index and Rumelt's categories.
• Vertical integration: value added to sales ratio, input-output tables (Adelman), the proportion of intermediate output sold internally (Maddigan).
• Merger performance: event studies (abnormal returns), accounting-ratio studies (pre-post profitability), and concentration changes.
Policy
Competition law reviews mergers: Competition Act 2002 in India with CCI scrutinises combinations above thresholds.
Conclusion
These strategies reflect efficiency, market power and managerial motives; welfare effects depend on whether efficiencies outweigh competition loss.